; jobshop-possible.pol — CAN every job finish?
;
; The shop is not here. It lives in ../scheduling.lib.pol, which both job shop
; scenarios load: three jobs, three machines, routings in a cycle, and the
; blocking rule. This file is only what may HAPPEN, which is the one thing the
; pair disagrees about.
;
; UNTIMED, on purpose. There is no clock in this model — the library offers
; one and this file never touches it, exactly as queens-unordered.pol declines
; the cursor its library declares. So this space holds ORDERS of events and
; not durations, which is the cheap question: does a schedule exist at all?
; ../jobshop-best/ pays for the clock and asks which schedule is shortest.
;
; The answer is the interesting part. Every job CAN finish — and it is not the
; case that every job can ALWAYS still finish. Three moves are enough to reach
; a situation where each job holds one machine and waits for the next, in a
; cycle, and nothing can move again. That is a `live` question, and `live` is
; what finds it.

(load "../libraries/scheduling.lib.pol")
(use shop)
(initial start)

; A job takes its first machine.
(form (enters J N)
  => (transition N
       (when (and (at-stage J queued) (idle J.first)))
       (do (set J.first.held-by J) (set J.stage on-first))))

; …then its second, WITHOUT letting go of the first until it has it. That is
; the blocking rule, and `hand-over` is where it is written down.
(form (advances J N)
  => (transition N
       (when (and (at-stage J on-first) (idle J.second)))
       (hand-over J J.first J.second (set J.stage on-second))))

; …and leaves, freeing the machine it held. A third phase, because a finished
; job that never released its last machine would block the shop for a reason
; that has nothing to do with scheduling.
(form (leaves J N)
  => (transition N
       (when (at-stage J on-second))
       (do (vacate J.second.held-by) (set J.stage done))))

(form (job-of J E A L) => (enters J E) (advances J A) (leaves J L))

(job-of a a-enters a-advances a-leaves)
(job-of b b-enters b-advances b-leaves)
(job-of c c-enters c-advances c-leaves)
